{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miscalibration: in downtrends the model mean-reverts upward too aggressively (predicts rebounds that don’t materialize). Reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 2–4 weeks show a clear negative slope.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: weeks 2–4 show the largest overestimation (often +5 to +12 EUR). Use a piecewise correction: minimal adjustment days 1–5, moderate days 6–10, strong days 11–30.",
    "Volatility overshoot after small rallies: when the model projects a mid-horizon bump (local peak around days ~7–15), realized path is flatter/lower. Damp forecast peaks (cap local increases) and smooth the trajectory.",
    "Downside underreaction: realized declines continue longer/deeper than forecast (model ‘lags’ the selloff). Add a momentum term that pulls forecasts down when recent returns are negative for multiple consecutive days."
  ]
}
